#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185023} :activity/id #uuid "2840c435-d873-5586-9c7c-6d7036b356b1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050986 :topic/id #uuid "772caedd-5534-5223-bec5-a96cc56f64c5" :topic/math-academy-id 3839} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184143 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184053 :question/id #uuid "5da3337e-31d2-5f2d-b236-60088b4bf64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3524"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048461}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552481}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"} :step/id #uuid "ac838c11-0bfd-54e8-b4e8-850cec75401e" :step/math-academy-id 11781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185350 :tutorial/content "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for cosine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {3}{5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = -\\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant?\n\nFirst, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\cos(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\right) \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) +  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right) \\[5pt]\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\dfrac{3}{15} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}+3}{15}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "75e177a6-4974-5699-b211-c562c0b8bafe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6209 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c28e4265-3725-531e-aa72-0bd495eb57af" :step/math-academy-id 37640 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184135 :question/id #uuid "8877f6b8-8914-5022-9ec6-d4e8e0d3d159" :question/math-academy-id "e-9992"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050714}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302226} {:db/id #edb/ref 